];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Are ravens common in Alaska?', a: 'Yes. The Common Raven is abundant and widespread throughout Alaska — from the temperate rainforests of the southeast (where it is closely associated with Tlingit and Haida culture) to the boreal forests of the interior (around Fairbanks and the Tanana valley) to the Arctic tundra of the North Slope. Ravens are year-round residents in Alaska, among the few birds that remain through the severe interior winters. They are closely associated with human settlements in winter, exploiting food waste.' },
  { q: 'What do Alaska ravens eat in winter?', a: 'Alaska ravens in winter are highly opportunistic. Major winter food sources include: carrion from moose, caribou, and other large mammals that die in the cold; food scraps around human communities and roadsides; fish carcasses near rivers and coastal areas; and cache retrieval — ravens cache food extensively in autumn and retrieve it in winter. In the most extreme interior winter conditions, ravens from communal roosts near towns (Fairbanks, Anchorage, Juneau) forage almost entirely around human infrastructure.' },
  { q: 'What is the significance of the raven in Tlingit culture?', a: 'In Tlingit culture (and those of the Haida, Tsimshian, and other Northwest Coast peoples), Raven (Yéil in Tlingit) is the supreme Trickster-Creator figure. Raven stole the sun, moon, stars, and fire for humanity; he released the first humans from a clamshell; he created rivers and lakes. The raven is not only a trickster but also an ambiguous moral force — clever, greedy, creative, and transformative. The Raven moiety is one of the two fundamental social divisions of Tlingit society, encompassing roughly half the people.' },
];
