    <h1>Ravens in Alaska: Ecology, Population, and Indigenous Traditions</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Alaska supports one of the world's highest raven densities — birds are present year-round from the rainforests of the southeast to the Arctic tundra of the North Slope. <strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The raven is the central Trickster-Creator in Tlingit, Haida, and other Northwest Coast and Arctic Indigenous traditions — a figure who stole the sun and released humanity from a clamshell. The Raven moiety is a fundamental social division in Tlingit society.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Population and Ecology</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens occupy every major Alaskan habitat type year-round:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Southeast Alaska (rainforest coast):</strong> densest populations associated with the temperate rainforest, intertidal zone, and human settlements. Ravens forage on marine life, salmon carcasses, and food scraps from fishing communities.</li>
      <li><strong>Interior Alaska (boreal forest and taiga):</strong> breeding pairs in boreal forest; large communal winter roosts near towns. Fairbanks ravens are famous for surviving temperatures below −40°C, roosting on warm structures and exploiting dumpsters and food waste.</li>
      <li><strong>Arctic Alaska (tundra):</strong> breeding pairs use the few cliff sites and permafrost outcrops on the North Slope. Ravens are closely associated with human camps and infrastructure in this otherwise food-poor landscape.</li>
      <li><strong>Winter aggregations:</strong> non-breeding and territorial ravens alike concentrate around towns in the extreme interior winters — Fairbanks regularly has roosts of hundreds of birds.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Winter Survival at the Extremes</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens in interior Alaska have been extensively studied for cold-tolerance and behavioural adaptation. Key adaptations include:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Dense plumage with high insulation value — ravens can maintain body temperature at temperatures below −40°C with appropriate food intake.</li>
      <li>Increased foraging time and reduced activity in extreme cold — minimising heat loss through behaviour.</li>
      <li>Heavy use of food caches established in autumn; food caching is especially extensive in Alaskan ravens before winter.</li>
      <li>Communal roosting — large groups at sheltered warm sites (near power plants, buildings, in dense spruce stands) reduce individual heat loss through clustering.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Raven in Tlingit and Northwest Coast Culture</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The Raven (Yéil) is the supreme cultural hero and trickster of Tlingit oral tradition — and of the closely related Haida (Yáahl), Tsimshian, and other Northwest Coast peoples:<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Stealing light:</strong> in the most widespread Raven creation myth, Raven steals the sun, moon, and stars from a great chief who keeps them in boxes, releasing them into the sky for all humanity.</li>
      <li><strong>Releasing humanity:</strong> in one widely told version, Raven finds the first humans trapped inside a giant clamshell and coaxes or tricks them into emerging into the world.</li>
      <li><strong>Creating geography:</strong> Raven makes rivers, lakes, mountains, and other landscape features through trickery or transformation throughout the cycle of stories.</li>
      <li><strong>The Raven moiety:</strong> Tlingit society is divided into two clans (moieties) — Raven (Yéil) and Eagle (Ch'áak'). The moiety system regulates marriage, ceremony, and mutual obligation. Roughly half of all Tlingit people are Ravens.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

